  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold pays no coupon. Falling after-inflation bond returns reduce its opportunity cost. In the debt-cycle template, spending financed by money and credit outruns output and raises prices.",
   "data": "Three forces matter: hiring strengthened to 165,000, complicating easier policy; real yields fell to 2.60%, supporting gold, but that observation predates payrolls; breakeven inflation eased to 2.23%. Unlike a money-printing inflation episode, positive real yields and resilient hiring still constrain easing.",
   "unsustainable": "Debt persistently outgrowing receipts absorbs fiscal capacity. Spending cuts, restructuring, transfers or monetary easing can redistribute the burden; none is established by one jobs release.",
   "would_change": "Tuesday’s CPI at 21:30 HKT could alter the inflation and rate outlook. A sustained fall in real yields would strengthen gold’s monetary channel; firmer real returns would weaken it.",
   "check": "Debt-cycle read checked (GPT-6.1 Sol): 9 of 9 checks passed."
  },
